## 4. The UI

Landscape, dark, laid out like a car head unit rather than a phone app: a **104dp left
navigation rail**, a 60dp status bar (clock, link chip, outside temperature, radio
glyphs), and gradient cards with a 10%-aqua hairline.

It is a direct implementation of the hi-fi mockups in [branding/mockups/](branding/mockups/)
— open `02-dashboard.html` in a browser and the app should look like it. `warivo.css` and
`ui/theme/Theme.kt` hold the same palette and geometry and **must be changed together**;
the CSS says so at the top, and it is the only thing keeping the mockups honest.

- **Drive** — a fixed 560dp speed cluster (270° gradient ring, big numeral, gear mode
  pill) with a telltale tile strip; then battery (SoC ring + estimated range with a
  segmented meter) and a 3×2 tile grid: power, trip, odometer, pack temp, current, avg
  speed. Speed gets the whole left column because it is the only value read while moving;
  the tiles are what you check at a stop.
- **Map** — full-bleed MapLibre + raster OSM following the phone's GPS, with a floating
  speed/position card and a recentre button.
- **Music** — player column beside output and queue cards, so changing track never hides
  what is playing.
- **Search** — the mockup's landing page (wordmark, pill field, shortcut chips, recents);
  searching swaps in a Google-only WebView.
- **Setup** — link state, beep, kiosk toggle, escape hatch. No mockup; same card system.

Panels do **not** swipe. The map and the search WebView both consume horizontal drags, so
a pager would fight them on exactly the two panels where it matters.

Three deliberate deviations from the mockups: the script wordmark falls back to the
platform cursive face rather than bundling Kaushan Script; the ring's glow is a wide faint
arc rather than a real blur; and album art is a gradient tile, because real artwork needs
an image-loading dependency. The map's turn-by-turn and ETA cards are not built at all —
they need routing and a destination.

## 5. Things that will bite you

Learned while writing this; all are commented at the relevant line.

- **BLE MTU is not optional.** A telemetry frame is ~140 bytes, but the default BLE MTU of
  23 caps a notification at 20. Without `requestMtu(247)` *before* service discovery,
  every frame arrives truncated and unparseable — which looks exactly like broken
  firmware. This is the single most likely cause of "the node sends nothing".
- **One GATT operation at a time.** Android silently drops a write issued while another is
  outstanding, so the CCCD subscribe and all writes go through a queue. Without it, 1 Hz
  GPS writes randomly eat the subscribe and telemetry never starts.
- **Locale breaks JSON.** GPS writes are formatted with `Locale.US`; on a phone set to a
  comma-decimal locale, the default would hand the firmware `{"lat":26,846700}`.
- **Trip distance comes from the odometer delta**, not from integrating speed, so a dropped
  BLE link costs no distance. A negative delta is read as a node reboot, not as travel.
- **Common ground or nothing.** ESP32 ground must tie to battery negative, or every ADC
  reading is meaningless.
- **Never drive a scooter line.** The taps in `audit.md` are read-only. Injecting voltage
  onto a throttle signal could command unintended acceleration.

